<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  Shared by widget_timer.xml and widget_timer_wallpaper.xml. Those two layouts
  exist only because RemoteViews cannot call setShadowLayer — it dispatches
  single-argument remotable methods, and a shadow takes four — so the shadow has
  to be declared in XML rather than chosen at draw time. Everything else about the
  two is identical and lives here, where it cannot drift.
-->
<resources>

    <style name="WidgetName">
        <item name="android:layout_width">match_parent</item>
        <item name="android:layout_height">wrap_content</item>
        <item name="android:ellipsize">end</item>
        <item name="android:gravity">center</item>
        <item name="android:maxLines">1</item>
        <item name="android:textSize">@dimen/widget_label_text_min</item>
    </style>

    <!--
      A fixed-height slot rather than wrap_content, so the text has a bounded box to sit
      in; tabular figures stop the text jittering as a ticking clock rolls through digits
      of different widths.

      Fixed, never weighted. A weighted slot absorbs every pixel the labels leave, and
      the value can only grow to its maximum — so on any cell taller than the rows need,
      the surplus lands as empty space *inside* the value's box, splitting the name off
      the top of the number and the date off the bottom. A bounded box leaves the surplus
      outside the rows, where the root's centre gravity turns it into margin and the
      three lines stay together. WidgetRenderer sets the real height per variant; see
      metricsFor.

      No auto-sizing here, and the size is not declared either: WidgetRenderer works it
      out per variant and sets it. Auto-sizing searches for the largest size that fits a
      box in a given number of lines, and that is one rule short — it will take a size
      whose only way to fit is to break `months,` across two lines, because a box says
      nothing about where a line may end. The renderer's search is the same one with that
      third rule added, so it has to be the search that decides.
    -->
    <style name="WidgetValue">
        <item name="android:layout_width">match_parent</item>
        <item name="android:layout_height">@dimen/widget_value_height</item>
        <item name="android:fontFeatureSettings">tnum</item>
        <item name="android:gravity">center</item>
        <item name="android:maxLines">1</item>
        <item name="android:textSize">@dimen/widget_value_text_min</item>
        <item name="android:textStyle">bold</item>
    </style>

    <!--
      The ticking value keeps auto-sizing, and has to. A `Chronometer` writes its own
      text once a second in the launcher's process, and the string it writes can grow —
      a count-up crossing an hour goes from `59:59` to `1:00:00` — with nothing asking
      this app to redraw, and a count-up with no calendar units above it is never redrawn
      at all. A size fixed at build time is a size that fits until it does not, so the
      only thing that can size this row is the row itself.

      The cost is the rule the search adds: a ticking value on a cell too narrow for one
      of its words can still be broken mid-word. Its head is the calendar part alone —
      what is left after the clock takes hours, minutes and seconds — so it is a word or
      two rather than the six a static value can carry.
    -->
    <style name="WidgetValue.Ticking">
        <item name="android:autoSizeMaxTextSize">@dimen/widget_value_text_max</item>
        <item name="android:autoSizeMinTextSize">@dimen/widget_value_text_min</item>
        <item name="android:autoSizeStepGranularity">1sp</item>
        <item name="android:autoSizeTextType">uniform</item>
    </style>

    <style name="WidgetFooter">
        <item name="android:layout_width">match_parent</item>
        <item name="android:layout_height">wrap_content</item>
        <item name="android:ellipsize">end</item>
        <item name="android:gravity">center</item>
        <item name="android:maxLines">1</item>
        <item name="android:textSize">@dimen/widget_label_text_min</item>
    </style>

    <!--
      The wallpaper variants add a shadow. A photo can be bright at one end of a
      line of text and dark at the other, so neither colour alone is enough; the
      shadow is what lets a single choice survive that.
    -->
    <style name="WidgetName.OnWallpaper">
        <item name="android:shadowColor">@color/widget_text_shadow</item>
        <item name="android:shadowDx">0</item>
        <item name="android:shadowDy">1</item>
        <item name="android:shadowRadius">4</item>
    </style>

    <style name="WidgetValue.OnWallpaper">
        <item name="android:shadowColor">@color/widget_text_shadow</item>
        <item name="android:shadowDx">0</item>
        <item name="android:shadowDy">2</item>
        <item name="android:shadowRadius">6</item>
    </style>

    <style name="WidgetValue.Ticking.OnWallpaper">
        <item name="android:shadowColor">@color/widget_text_shadow</item>
        <item name="android:shadowDx">0</item>
        <item name="android:shadowDy">2</item>
        <item name="android:shadowRadius">6</item>
    </style>

    <style name="WidgetFooter.OnWallpaper">
        <item name="android:shadowColor">@color/widget_text_shadow</item>
        <item name="android:shadowDx">0</item>
        <item name="android:shadowDy">1</item>
        <item name="android:shadowRadius">4</item>
    </style>
</resources>
